> 
> ## 4. How to Handle the “Contract” So It Feels OK
> 
> You can frame this clearly for your son, in simple language:
> 
> > Varkash: “I chose to be a champion. I signed a contract: I fight, they get a share of my winnings. But I don’t like Korga. I’d rather fight for someone **brave and fun**, like you.”  
> > “You’re not my owner. You’re my **captain**. You lead, I smash.”
> 
> If Austin leans into “slave” language, you can gently rephrase:
> 
> - “He’s sworn to defend you because you paid his contract. You’re like his **boss in battle**, not his owner.”
> - “If you ever treat him too badly, the contract magic might even break.” (This helps you course‑correct later if needed.)
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 5. Mechanics: Varkash as an NPC Companion
> 
> Simplify him for a 6‑year‑old game:
> 
> - Use something like a **CR 1–2 dragonborn gladiator**:
>   - AC: 16, HP: 30–40.
>   - Greatsword or spear: +5 to hit, 1d10+3 damage.
>   - Dragon Breath (red): 15‑ft cone, 2d6 fire, Dex save half, 1/day.
> - On Ekemon’s turn, ask:
>   - “What do *you* do?”  
>   - Then: “And what do you tell **Varkash** to do? Attack, guard you, or do something cool?”
> 
> Always keep options to **2–3 simple choices** for Varkash.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 6. Ending the Session
> 
> Once he’s got Varkash:
> 
> > “You and Varkash step out of the arena together.  
> > Above, you hear a distant dragon roar from the cliffs.  
> > In your pack, the Titan Seed beats in time with your footsteps—and now with the heavy steps of a dragonborn champion at your side.  
> > Your journey to become a Titan… has a new ally.”
> 
> Then you can ask:  
> “Next time, do you want to go **up the cliffs toward the dragons**, or **deeper into the town to learn its secrets**?”
> 
