> You’ve got a lot of freedom here, but here’s a clean, 5e‑ish way to do it that will feel fair and keep him leveling at a nice pace.
> 
> I’ll assume:
> 
> - **Red rock‑lizard** ≈ reskinned **giant lizard / guard drake–ish** (low CR).
> - **Arena fighter** ≈ CR 1–2 “named” humanoid (Flame‑Fist Rokan or similar).
> 
> ### By-the-book-ish XP
> 
> Use the 5e CR table as a guide and then adjust for “solo, 6‑year‑old, dramatic moment”.
> 
> **Red lizard on the road**
> 
> - Treat as **CR 1/2** or **CR 1**.
> - XP values:
>   - CR 1/2 → **100 XP**
>   - CR 1   → **200 XP**
> 
> I’d recommend:  
> - Call it **CR 1** for drama: **200 XP**.
> 
> **Arena opponent**
> 
> - If it’s a big moment and a solid fight, set at **CR 2**.
> - XP values:
>   - CR 1 → 200 XP
>   - CR 2 → 450 XP
> 
> I’d recommend:
> - Make the arena foe **CR 2**: **450 XP**.
> 
> That gives:
> 
> - **Red lizard:** 200 XP  
> - **Arena victory:** 450 XP  
> - **Total:** **650 XP**
> 
> For a solo PC, that’s a chunky but reasonable reward for “two signature fights and gaining a champion.”
> 
> ### Quick sanity check vs. level 3
> 
> 5e XP thresholds per character:
> - Level 2 → 3: needs **300 XP**
> - Level 3 → 4: needs **900 XP**
> 
> If he just reached **level 3**, then:
> 
> - +650 XP from this adventure moves him most of the way toward level 4 without overshooting too fast, and you can sprinkle small extra XP (roleplay, clever ideas, side quest) to top it off when you’re ready.
> 
> ### If you want it simpler
> 
> If you don’t want to track numbers tightly, you can just rule:
> 
> - “You beat the lizard: **that’s worth 1 ‘star’ of experience.**”
> - “You win the arena fight and gain a champion: **that’s 2 stars.**”
> - “3 stars → gain a level.”
> 
> Then secretly map those stars to XP later if you want, but at your son’s age, concrete “stars toward next level” is often easier than bookkeeping.
> 
